      "text": "To find the length of the hypotenuse \\(h\\) of the right triangle, we start by identifying the lengths of the legs. The legs are given as \\(\\log_4 27\\) and \\(\\log_2 9\\).\n\nFirst, we convert \\(\\log_4 27\\) to a base 2 logarithm. We use the change of base formula:\n\\[\n\\log_4 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 4} = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{2} = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\nSo, the first leg of the triangle is \\(\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\\).\n\nNext, we convert \\(\\log_2 9\\) to a base 4 logarithm. We use the change of base formula again:\n\\[\n\\log_2 9 = \\frac{\\log_4 9}{\\log_4 2} = \\frac{2 \\log_4 3}{1} = 2 \\log_4 3.\n\\]\nSo, the second leg of the triangle is \\(2 \\log_4 3\\).\n\nSince \\(\\log_4 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 3}{\\log_2 4} = \\frac{\\log_2 3}{2}\\), we have:\n\\[\n2 \\log_4 3 = 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2 3}{2} = \\log_2 3.\n\\]\nThus, the second leg is \\(\\log_2 3\\).\n\nNow, we use the Pythagorean theorem to find the hypotenuse \\(h\\):\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} \\right)^2 + (\\log_2 3)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{9 (\\log_2 3)^2}{4} + (\\log_2 3)^2}.\n\\]\nWe can factor out \\((\\log_2 3)^2\\) from the expression under the square root:\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{9}{4} + 1 \\right) (\\log_2 3)^2} = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{9}{4} + \\frac{4}{4} \\right) (\\log_2 3)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4} (\\log_2 3)^2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{13} \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\nWe need to find \\(4^h\\). Substituting the expression for \\(h\\), we get:\n\\[\n4^h = 4^{\\frac{\\sqrt{13} \\log_2 3}{2}} = \\left( 4^{\\log_2 3} \\right)^{\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}} = 3^{\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}}.\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3^{\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}}}.\n\\]",
